{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is more probable, but as a non-inductive, methodological rule to select the theory with the highest 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) and greatest empirical content, accepting the cost that this preference offers no guarantee of future success and relies on a metaphysical faith in the uniformity of nature which remains unjustified by logic.","counterconditions":["If corroboration is shown to logically entail a higher probability of future survival, the distinction collapses into induction.","If 'verisimilitude' cannot be defined without relying on inductive assumptions about the structure of reality, the defense fails.","If the preference for corroborated theories yields no better practical results than random selection, the methodological rule is empty."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'probability' (logical likelihood based on past frequency) and 'corroboration' (a report on how well a theory has withstood severe tests). By preferring the theory with higher content that has survived stricter tests, scientists maximize the potential for learning from error (falsification) and approximate truth (verisimilitude) without asserting that the theory is statistically more likely to be true. This avoids induction by treating the choice as a rational convention for progress rather than a logical derivation of future reliability.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}